== Living Together in Love

=== §. 10. #emph[Of mutual love between man and wife.]

So much for those common-mutual duties which tend to preserve the very #emph[being] of marriage, and are in that respect absolutely necessary. The other common-mutual duties (though they are not of such absolute necessity as the former) are in their kind necessary for the good condition of marriage, and for better preserving that knot. If they are not performed, the end and right use of marriage will be perverted, and that state made comfortless and very burdensome. The first of these is #emph[love.] A loving mutual affection must pass between husband and wife, or else no duty will be well performed: this is the ground of all the rest. In some respects #emph[love] belongs properly and distinctively to a husband, as #footnote[Treatise 4, §. 2.] I intend to show when I come to speak of a husband’s particular duties. But #emph[love] is also required of wives, and they are commanded “to love their husbands,” just as husbands are commanded “to love their wives.” #footnote[φιλάνδρους εἶναι. Titus 2:4. Col 3:19.] So it is a common-mutual duty belonging to husband and wife alike: and that is true wedlock, when man and wife are linked together by the bond of love. Under #emph[love] all other duties #footnote[#emph[Hoc verum est coniugium, quando vinculo charitatis sunt concatenati. Chrys. hom.] 45. #emph[in Gen.]] are included, for without it no duty can be well performed. “Love is the fulfillment of the law,”#footnote[Rom 13:10.] that is, the very life of all the duties which the law requires. It is “the bond of perfect unity,”#footnote[Col 3:14.] which binds together all the duties that pass between one party and the other. Where #emph[love] abounds, all duties will be performed readily and cheerfully. Where #emph[love] is lacking, every duty will either be wholly neglected, or performed so carelessly that it might as well not be performed at all. For this reason the apostle commands, “Do everything #footnote[1 Cor 16:14.] in love.” As #emph[love] provokes the party in whom it rules to do all the good it can, so it stirs up the party loved to repay good for good. It is like fire, which is not only hot in itself, but also conveys heat into what is near it: and from this arises a reflection of heat from one to the other. Note how wonderfully this is set out between Christ and his spouse in the Song of Solomon; and it is further shown in the examples of all the good husbands and wives noted in Scripture: they bore a very loving affection for one another.

Though #emph[love] is a general duty which everyone owes to another, #footnote[Matt 5:44.] even to his enemy, yet the more closely God has linked any together, the more they are bound to this duty, and the more they must abound in it. But of all others #footnote[Treatise 1, §. 79, 80.] man and wife are most closely and firmly linked together. Of all others, therefore, they are most bound to it, and that in the highest degree possible, even like Jonathan’s love, who loved David #footnote[1 Sam 18:1. Prov 18:22.] “as himself.” Solomon says, “He who finds a wife finds a good thing and obtains favor from the #smallcaps[Lord].” By the rule of relation this is also true of a husband: “She who finds a husband finds a good thing and obtains favor from the #smallcaps[Lord].” Man and wife, therefore, are to each other a special pledge of God’s favor, and in this respect are to be loved above all others under God. If this is the ground (as it ought to be) of their mutual #footnote[What is said in #emph[Treatise] 4, §. 2, 9, 11, of a husband’s love and entire affection may also be applied in some respects to a wife, and so serve to amplify this common-mutual duty of love.] love, their love will be fervent and constant. Neither the lack nor the withering of any outward attractions, such as beauty, figure, family, friends, riches, honors or the like, will withhold or withdraw, extinguish or diminish their love. Nor will any excellencies of nature or grace in other husbands and wives draw their hearts from their own to those others. Nor again will the love of a former yokefellow, dead and gone, lessen in the least the love of the living mate.

I have mentioned this instance all the more because in many #footnote[Love in a later marriage to be as entire as in the former.] who are far from setting their affection on another’s flesh, the love of a former husband or wife who has died is so firmly fixed in their heart that they can never again love any other so entirely. Those who are so minded are not fit to be joined to another yokefellow after they are released from one. If they marry again and show such a mind, they plainly show that they regard this or that person more than God’s ordinance. By God’s ordinance man and wife are bound to one another no longer than they live together. Death is an absolute severing, and makes an utter dissolution of the marriage bond. “If her husband #footnote[Rom 7:2, 3.] dies, she is released from the law of marriage,” so that she may take another man. The same liberty is also given to the man. Being now free, if they marry another (that other being now a true husband or wife), their love must be as entire toward that other as it was toward the former; indeed, more entire, if there was any defect in the former. For as children married out of their parents’ house must not keep such a love for their parents as swallows up their love for the party to whom they are married, but must, according to the law, leave father and mother and hold fast to their yokefellow, so neither must the love of a former husband or wife prevail when they are married to another. This other must be held fast as closely as if they had never been joined to a former one. The living husband or wife is the present pledge of God’s favor. He is now your #emph[own] husband, and she is now your #emph[own] wife, and not the party who is dead. I do not deny that the memory of a virtuous husband or wife ought to be precious to the surviving party, for “the memory of the righteous is a blessing.” #footnote[Prov 10:7.] But as the virtue of a deceased person must not be buried with the dead body, so neither must the person be kept above ground with the memory of his or her virtue. This is done in a sense when love of the deceased party either takes away or lessens the love of the living. This is to give the dead dominion over the living, which is more than the law commands.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual hatred, contrary to love.]

There is a generation of so sour and crooked a disposition #footnote[See Treatise 3, §. 8, #emph[&] Treatise 4, §. 10.] that they cannot love, but rather hate one another, because they are man and wife. For many husbands who have wives, and wives who have husbands, worthy in every way to be loved, will nevertheless say, to the astonishment of their hearers, “I have indeed a good husband, but I cannot love him,” or “I have a good wife, but I cannot love her.” And when they are asked for a reason, they do not hesitate to reply openly and shamelessly, “I think I could love him if he were not my husband,” or “I think I could love her if she were not my wife.” O more than monstrous impudence! Is not this directly to oppose God’s ordinance, and the order which he has set between man and woman? Is it not to trample God’s favor underfoot? Even if there were nothing else to move love but this, that #emph[such a one is your husband,] or #emph[such a one is your wife,] yet this should be motive enough. And shall this be the ground of your hatred? Assuredly such a spirit is a plainly diabolical spirit, contrary to the spirit which is from above; and if it is not cast out, it will cast those whom it possesses into the fire of hell.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of mutual peace between man and wife.]

Among other means of maintaining an inward loving affection between man and wife, #emph[outward mutual peace,] concord and agreement is one of the chief. So the apostle exhorts us “to keep the unity of the spirit through the bond #footnote[Eph 4:3.] of peace”: for peace is a bond that ties one to another, and makes them as one, even one in spirit; just as, on the other side, outward discord divides men’s spirits. We are commanded to “pursue peace with everyone”:#footnote[Heb 12:14.] how much more, of all persons, ought husbands to have peace with their wives, and wives with their husbands! They are nearer than brothers and sisters. See then how good and pleasant a thing it is for them to dwell together in unity. #footnote[§. 14. Prov 21:9.] Dwell together they must: but without peace there is no dwelling together. “Better to dwell on a corner of the roof than to share a house with a quarrelsome wife.” Persons at variance would be far better out of sight and apart than present together. Man and wife must not be out of sight and apart; at peace, therefore, #footnote[#emph[Portus est mulier, & maximum securitatis remedium. Si ventis & fluctibus portum liberaueris ex foro reuersus tranquillè viues. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] they must be. Mutual peace between them is a great refreshment to their minds when they have been battered by the discords of others. It is said that a wife is in this respect like a haven to a man (and how much more a man to his wife!). If the haven is calm, and free from storms and tempests, what a refreshment it will be to the sailor who has been tossed on the sea by winds and waves!

For maintaining peace:

1\. All offenses must be avoided as far as possible. #footnote[Directions for maintaining peace between man and wife.] The husband must watch over himself so that he gives no offense to his wife, and so must the wife on her side. Offenses cause contentions.

2\. When an offense is given by one party, it must #footnote[1\. Avoid offense.] not be taken by the other, but rather passed over; and then peace will not be broken. The second blow makes #footnote[2\. Do not take offense.] the fray. #footnote[3\. Offer reconciliation.]

3\. If both are angered together, the fire is likely to be the greater; so they must both labor all the faster to put it out. Wrath must not lie in bed#footnote[Eph 4:26.] with two such bedfellows; nor must they part beds for wrath’s sake. So that this fire may be quenched the sooner, they must both strive to be the first to offer reconciliation. The glory belongs to those who begin first, for they are most properly the blessed peacemakers.#footnote[Matt 5:9.] Not to accept peace when it is offered is worse than heathenish; but when wrath is kindled, to seek reconciliation is the duty of a Christian, and a grace that comes from above.

4\. Neither children, nor servants, nor anyone else in the family must be #footnote[4\. Take no one’s part against the other.] backed by the one against the other. The husband’s siding with anyone in the house against his wife, or the wife’s against her husband, is a common cause of contention between man and wife.

5\. They must refrain from taunting one another #footnote[5\. Make no comparisons.] with the husbands or wives of other persons, or with their own former husbands or wives (if they have had any before). Comparisons of this kind are very odious. They stir up much anger and cause great contentions.

6\. Above all they must beware of rash and unjust jealousy, #footnote[6\. Do not be jealous.] which is the bane of marriage, and the greatest cause of discontent that can arise between man and wife. Jealous persons are ready to pick quarrels and to look for occasions of discord: they will take every word, look, action and gesture in the worst sense, and so take offense where none is given. Once jealousy is kindled, it is like a flaming fire that can hardly be put out.#footnote[Prov 6:34, 35.] It makes the party whom it possesses implacable.

7\. In all things that are consistent with a good conscience they must endeavor to please one another, and each #footnote[7\. Please one another.] of them must allow their own will to be crossed rather than cause discontent to the other. Saint Paul notes this as a common #footnote[1 Cor 7:33, 34.] mutual duty belonging to them both, and expresses their care for it by a word that signifies more than ordinary #footnote[μεριμνᾶν, quasi μερίζειν τὸν νοῦν.] care, and implies a #emph[dividing of the mind] into various thoughts, casting this way and that way and every way how best to please the other.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of contentions between man and wife.]

Contrary to mutual peace are #emph[contentions] between man and wife. They are all too frequent in most families, and by them #footnote[#emph[Cùm vir & vxor domi dissentiunt, nihilo melius naui iactata domus illa disponitur, cùm gubernator aduersus prorae directorem dissentiat. Chrys. loc. citat.] Gen 13:8.] the common good is much hindered. Discord between man and wife in a house is like contention between the master and the pilot in a ship: is not great danger and much injury justly to be feared from it? We heard before that a man is like a haven to his wife, and she to him. Now we find by experience that if the haven is stormy, it is much more troublesome and dangerous to the sailor than the wide sea. Therefore let man and wife be of the same mind toward one another as Abraham was toward Lot, and when occasions of discord arise, say, “Let there be no strife between you and me, for we are man and wife: no longer two, but one flesh.”

=== §. 14. #emph[Of husbands and wives dwelling together.]

From a mutual affection of #emph[love] comes #emph[a mutual provident care] in husband and wife for one another. In handling it we will first note the #emph[means] by which their mutual provident care may be better carried out and shown, and then the #emph[matter] with which it must be concerned. The means, in one word, is #emph[cohabitation.] For it is a duty that man and wife dwell together. The phrase used in setting out the woman’s creation (he #emph[built] a woman, which hints at the setting up of #footnote[יבן #emph[Vide Musc. in] Gen 2:22. See #emph[Treatise] 1, §. 79.] a family) implies as much. So does the law of marriage, by which a man is commanded to “leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife,” that is, to go out of his father’s house and dwell with his wife. And so does this phrase (“forget #footnote[Ps 45:10.] your father’s house”), taken from the duty of a wife and applied mystically to the church. Saint Peter expressly charges husbands #footnote[1 Pet 3:7.] to “dwell with their wives,” and Saint Paul lays it as #footnote[1 Cor 7:12, 13.] a charge on both husbands and wives not to leave one another, but to dwell together, even though one of them is an unbeliever. Surely it was conscience of this duty that made the #footnote[Judg 19:3.] Levite go after his wife, who had gone away from him, to bring her home again; and that made Jacob’s#footnote[Gen 31:14.] wives leave their father’s house and go with their husband. #footnote[συνοικοῦντες. συνοικῶν #emph[coniux.] σύνοικος #emph[vxor.]] The word in which Saint Peter sums up this duty is a title reserved for a husband, and a corresponding title is reserved for a wife: and the derivation of our two usual English words #emph[(husband, housewife)] is not much different.

The power which each has over the other’s body, and #footnote[Reasons for cohabitation.] the advantage which they give to Satan by living apart (both of which are expressly noted, 1 Cor 7:4, 5), show the necessity of this duty; and the many benefits that come from it further urge its rightness. By husbands’ and #footnote[Benefits of cohabitation.] wives’ dwelling together all marriage duties are better performed; mutual love is better bred, preserved and increased; the good gifts of each are better observed by the other; better help and relief are given by each to the other; and in short they are made more able to do good to one another, and to receive good from one another. Why then should they have any mind to live apart, unless they are forced by extraordinary occasions, such as captivity, close imprisonment, contagious sickness and the like? These are no faults of theirs, but crosses to be borne with grief; and earnest prayer, together with all other good means, is to be used to bring them together again. Indeed, if the imprisonment, banishment or other such absence #footnote[D. #emph[Fulke] against #emph[Rhem.] on 1 Cor 7. §. 6.] is such that one may come to the other if they will, the party who is free ought to go to the other, at least if that other asks for it.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of the reasons for which man and wife may live apart for a time.]

#emph[Question.] May there be any just causes for man and wife willingly to live apart?

#emph[Answer.] There neither can nor ought to be any cause for utterly forsaking one another, which is a kind of #footnote[§. 3.] #emph[desertion.] But there may be just causes for living apart for a time, such as:

1\. #emph[Weighty and urgent affairs] which concern the good of the church or commonwealth: as when a man is sent out to war, or on an embassy (in which case, though he may take his wife with him, he is not necessarily bound to, especially if the place he is sent to is far off, the journey there difficult and dangerous, and his stay there not long). When Reuben, Gad,#footnote[Num 32:26, 27.] and half the tribe of Manasseh crossed over the Jordan to help their brethren in their battles against the Canaanites, they left their wives behind them in their families. #footnote[2 Sam 11:3, 6.] When Uriah went to war, he left his wife at home. And #footnote[Exod 18:2.] when Moses was to bring Israel out of Egypt, his wife stayed at her father’s house.

2\. #emph[The main duties of their particular calling:] as of #emph[sailors,] who must often be at sea; #emph[merchants] who trade in other countries; #emph[lawyers] who attend the public courts of justice; #emph[courtiers,] who in their months or quarters attend on their prince; #emph[attendants] of women recovering from childbirth and of sick persons; and other #emph[nurses.]

Provided always that in these and other such cases there is #footnote[Absence, 1. not without consent. 1 Cor 7:5.] a joint and mutual consent of both parties. For if man and wife must not deprive one another for a time to give themselves to fasting and prayer “except by mutual consent,” much less may they live apart for any time, for lighter reasons, without #footnote[2\. Not with delight. #emph[Si vir ad peregrina contenderit, nulla longinquitas, nulla absentia complacitam minuat charitatem. Ambr. Hexaem. l.] 5. #emph[c.] 7.] consent. Provided also that they take no delight in living apart, but are rather grieved that they are forced to do so; and that, as evidence of this, they take every occasion they can to show their longing for one another by letters, messages, tokens and other such kindnesses, and return with all the speed they can. No distance or absence ought to lessen their mutual love in the least.

=== §. 16. #emph[Of the error of papists about a man and wife’s separation.] #footnote[#emph[Si quis dixerit Ecclesiam errare cùm ob multas causas separationem inter coniuges quoad thorum, seu cohabitationem, ad certum, incertumue tempus fieri posse decernit, Anathema sit. Concil. Trid.] §. 24. #emph[can.] 8. \[If anyone says that the Church errs when it decrees that, for many causes, separation between spouses as to bed or cohabitation may take place for a fixed or an indefinite time, let him be anathema.\]]

Contrary to the duty of #emph[cohabitation] is the doctrine of the papists, in which they teach that

#emph[Separation may be made between man and wife for many causes, from bed or cohabitation, for a certain or uncertain time.] The Council of Trent boldly pronounces #emph[anathema] on those who say #emph[the church errs in this.] If the many causes which they allege besides adultery are well weighed, we shall find them wholly without warrant from God’s word. #footnote[#emph[Bellarm. de Matrim. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 14.] They bring them under two heads: 1. #emph[Mutual consent.] 2. #emph[Demerit.]

#emph[By consent] (they say) #emph[of both married parties, in order to attain a greater and more perfect state, a consummated marriage may be loosed from bed and cohabitation.]

#emph[Answer.] 1. In marriage there is #emph[a covenant of God] (Prov 2:17) as well as of the two parties: the consent of the parties, therefore, is not sufficient to break it.

2\. No state in this world can be greater or more perfect than is fitting for married persons. Adam and Eve in their best state were married; and now “marriage is honorable in all.” #footnote[Heb 13:4.]

3\. The states which they count more perfect are either such as are not in man’s power #emph[(as perpetual self-control),] or such as may be carried out as well by married persons as by single persons #emph[(as ecclesiastical offices),] at least if they are such as are warranted by God’s word. High priests and other priests, all sorts of Levites, extraordinary prophets and apostles were married. What offices are greater than these?

The causes which, they say, make a separation for #emph[demerit] are: 1. #emph[Adultery.] 2. #emph[Departing from the Catholic faith.] 3. #emph[Soliciting or driving to sin.]

Concerning #footnote[See §. 5.] #emph[adultery,] we do not deny that it gives just cause for divorce; but we also say (as #footnote[Matt 5:32.] we have good warrant from Christ’s words) that it is the only cause of just divorce. For to make a separation for #emph[departing from the Catholic faith] #footnote[#emph[Fidei gratia vxorem ne eijcias.] \[Do not put away your wife for the faith’s sake.\] #emph[Chrys. hom.] 19. #emph[in] 1 Cor 7.] is directly contrary to the doctrine of Saint Paul and Saint Peter, 1 Cor 7:12, 13, 14. 1 Pet 3:1.

As for #emph[soliciting and driving to sin,] though it may be a cause to move a husband or a wife to walk more warily and #footnote[#emph[Si ferus moribus sit coniux, perferat illum vxor, nec vllam ob causam societatem, vinculumque dissoluere patiatur.] \[If the husband is savage in his ways, let the wife bear with him, and not for any cause allow their fellowship and bond to be dissolved.\] #emph[Basil. Hexaem. hom.] 7. Matt 5:29.] wisely, and in extremity to avoid each other’s company for a time, or to complain to the magistrate for relief (who may see fit to put the offending person in prison, or else keep them apart in some other way until the offender is reclaimed and brought to a better mind), yet it is no sufficient cause to dissolve marriage finally with respect to bed and cohabitation.

They urge that #emph[if the right eye causes one to stumble, it must be plucked out.]

#emph[Answer.] 1. That is only a metaphor, and may be applied in various other ways.

2\. The words are to be taken not absolutely but by way of comparison: #emph[rather pluck it out than be made to stumble by it.]

3\. #emph[Plucking out,] applied to the point in hand, may be done in many other ways than by dissolving marriage.

4\. That general prohibition (“anyone who divorces his wife #footnote[Matt 5:32.] … brings adultery upon her”), limited only by the exception of fornication, admits neither this nor any other such cause for dissolving marriage.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ unlawfully absenting themselves from one another.]

Contrary also to the duty of #emph[cohabitation] named above is the practice of many men who, living themselves in one place (say, at London), send their wives off to some country house, and there even cage them up like hawks, never caring to come to them; and they are merriest when their wives are farthest off. If their wives live at home, they will be away, taking their meals and their lodging where their wives cannot know. Their own house is like a prison to them: they are not well except when they are out of it. Of the same wicked notion and practice are many wives who, for no other reason than sheer looseness, eager in pursuing their pleasures and satisfying their lusts, gad out of their own houses by day, lie out of them by night, and stay in other company days and nights together; or at least are glad when their husbands have occasion to be away from home: not unlike the loose woman whom Solomon describes, Prov 7:10, etc. I speak of matters too famous, or rather infamous. I wish there were no just cause to rebuke this looseness. But let those who desire to approve themselves to God or man beware of these heinous and worse than heathenish vices. “Though you prostitute yourself, O Israel, may Judah #footnote[Hos 4:15.] avoid such guilt!”

Those come too near to the unlawful separation named above #footnote[The fault of man and wife in parting rooms and beds in the same house.] who, though they both live in one house, yet make that house like two houses by estranging themselves from one another: the man staying in one end of it, his wife in another. So they have separate rooms, separate tables, separate servants, everything separate. Or if the smallness of their house will not allow them to divide the other rooms like this, they will still have separate bedrooms, or at least separate beds: so that whoever calls them bedfellows only gives them a false name. In this way they rob each other of that due benevolence, the marriage duty which they owe one another; they expose themselves to the devil’s snares; they estrange their hearts more and more from one another; and they deprive themselves of the mutual comforts and helps which they might give to and receive from one another through the fellowship of marriage.
